Yes. I think nLab should be the center of the prior-art phase, with Hinton/Smith and the broader 4D tradition serving as historical background rather than the main target.

Why nLab is unusually valuable is that it is not just another isolated theory source. It functions as a **self-aware map of the modern mathematical lineage**: the Cross work already found explicit continuity there from Klein/Poincaré/Minkowski through Cartan geometry and first-order gravity into super-Cartan geometry, higher gauge theory, branes, higher-dimensional supergravity, and related structures. fileciteturn62file0L10-L10 That makes it much more useful than random keyword-matched papers for asking where SAT/H(s)H sits relative to an existing professional tradition.

And within nLab, **Schreiber/Hypothesis H looks like the most consequential comparator**, not because we've established that SAT/H(s)H reduces to it—we explicitly have not—but because it bundles several of the same broad mathematical neighborhoods into a coherent research program. Cross had already isolated the open question of whether any relationship would look like compactification, dimensional reduction, projection, quotient, restriction, coarse-graining, or something else entirely. fileciteturn62file0L10-L10

So I would organize the nLab pass in layers:

- first, reconstruct **their own lineage map** without trying to force SAT/H(s)H onto it;
- second, establish the chronology of Schreiber's work and Hypothesis H;
- third, inventory the actual mathematical objects and dependency structures involved;
- fourth, compare those against the **dated SAT/H(s)H record**, not the September-2026 reconstruction as a timeless whole;
- fifth, distinguish **shared deep tradition**, **specific antecedent**, **architectural overlap**, and **possible reduction/equivalence relation**.

The exposure boundary is also unusually clean here: Cross records direct nLab exposure as September 2026, after the relevant SAT/H(s)H structures were already documented, with the material immediately quarantined. So nLab is a **post hoc comparator and prior-art source**, not something we should silently treat as developmental input. fileciteturn62file7L80-L80

That makes nLab almost ideal for what we want: it lets us ask, with unusually good provenance hygiene, **how much of the territory SAT/H(s)H independently wandered into was already occupied, how it was organized there, and where the architectures genuinely diverge.**

